nb

nb (_nota bene_) is a small application and integration that allows me to build a note-taking system that is:
  * atomic
  * concept-based
  * highly linkable
  * portable to other devices
  * terminal-first

Specification

There is a folder F, which can contain markdown files M and other folders F creating a file tree `F = {M,F}`
such that a folder F'' in a folder F' in the folder F containing markdown file M'' is co-located as F'/F''/M''
in reference to F.  This is the `path`.

When writing text in M, a user can specify a _link_ to another file N by using its path in reference to M.
LINK == [[path]]. Using the earlier example, a file at path M, can link to M->M'' as [[F'/F''/M'']] and M'' can 
back-link M''->M as [[../../M]] where `..` implies walking backwards up a folder towards F. Another simple example is M and M_1 which exist both under F. M -> M_1 = [[M_1]].

Requirements

The application operates with a CLI and nvim interplay, that can index and map all links within F, and provide a trivial way to 
find and acess the linked note, view all links, and create a new link.

References

* https://notes.andymatuschak.org/, what gave me this idea.
* [Zettelkasten](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zettelkasten), the original idea of "webbed" or directed stricture of note-taking.
* [zk](https://github.com/zk-org/zk), a similar but not same implementation of some of the specification.

Usage 

  Build 

You must have go>1.22 installed. Then you can build `nb` and add it to your path. 
`nb init` will generate a `.nb` with a config (containing only nvim as editor default).

  Neovim

To set up in neovim, please add the nb.lua to your config (or prepend to rtp path) then 
`require('nb').setup()`. The basic commands (assuming telescope) are as follows:

  | Keys | Command | Action |
  |---|---|---|
  | `gf` | `:NbFollow` | Follow the link under the cursor |
  | `<leader>nf` | `:NbFind` | Find and open a note |
  | `<leader>nl` | `:NbLinks` | Follow an outgoing link |
  | `<leader>nb` | `:NbBacklinks` | Jump to a backlink's source occurrence |
  | `<leader>na` | `:NbAllLinks` | Jump to any link's source occurrence |
  | `<leader>ni` | `:NbInsert` | Insert a relative link to a selected note |

  CLI

The CLI offers a simple set of tools to work with.

```
nb list theory                    # Find notes by path substring
nb index                          # Whole graph as JSON
nb links                          # All active links
nb links concepts/note            # Outgoing links
nb backlinks concepts/note        # Incoming links
nb resolve concepts/note ../other # Absolute target of a source-relative link
nb open concepts/note             # Open an existing note
nb link concepts/note other       # Print [[../other]] for insertion
```

Footnotes 

I don't actually _like_ the markdown specification, and much rather plain text (.txt; .text; or nil)
